1298:| OD8 | <a id="od8"></a>Global state: immutable globals everywhere; mutable state only as declared cells in unsafe-marked modules, reached through capabilities; no `static mut` | D8; pending (TRUST-6, R-0509) | R-0509, R-0496; V6 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: Austral's capability model, extended. (1) Constants only: no mutable global state of any kind (no `static mut`, no cells); a constant is truly immutable, has no interior mutability and never contains a capability, token or handle. (2) Global resources are linear tokens minted at startup: `main` receives a root resource value (in the spirit of Austral's `RootCapability`) and splits it; hardware and runtime singletons (allocator arena, UART and MMIO, panic buffer, errno, C libraries with internal state) are produced or taken only by `trusted fn` wrappers returning or taking linear tokens, and a gate lists those wrappers. (3) Tokens are ordinary values: every effect shows as a token parameter, under the existing loan and exclusivity rules (OD1, OD2, OD15), with no separate effect system. (4) Interrupt handlers, FFI callbacks and future threads get only the tokens explicitly given to them, by installation, transfer or a lock granting a borrow. (5) No life before `main`, no cleanup protocol for global state, and abort runs nothing (OD10). (6) An implicit-context mechanism, if ergonomics later demands one, is a new owner decision, never a hidden global. The cell variant was rejected after the Codex and Grok reviews (interrupts, FFI re-entrancy, linear cell contents, effect polymorphism, concurrency). Now TRUST-8 to TRUST-10 (pending, R-0557) Revised 2026-11-01 – 11-02: items 2 and 3 are superseded by the authority decision (grants handed to `main`; `with(...)` plus resource values); item 5 stands ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)) |
1301:| OD11 | <a id="od11"></a>Casts: `as` only when lossless by value domain; every lossy conversion is a named function | T-CAST: ARITHMETIC_POLICY §8 (2026-10-21) and §4.5's value-domain rule; not implemented | exit 2k; the V1 increment that admits casts | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: `as` only when lossless by value domain; every lossy conversion is a named function |
1309:| OD19 | <a id="od19"></a>Outside the language list: the scope of [PROVABLE_V1](../legacy/docs/verification/PROVABLE_V1.md), that is, whether discarded statements, guards and ranges are admitted | open; it holds the model-coverage branch (`wip/model-coverage-3`) | that branch's landing; exit 2c's model-diff coverage | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: the provable subset admits discarded statements, guards and ranges, each entering as its Core semantics lands (this unblocks `wip/model-coverage-3`) **Revised 2026-11-03: the guards and ranges fragments were superseded by OD55 and OD56 (2026-10-31), which removed those forms; discarded statements remain subject to the decided discard rules.** |
1311:| OD21 | <a id="od21"></a>Representation rights of a parent over its child module (added 2026-10-25, from bug 262): when a parent module imports its child's newtype or struct, every stage lets the parent construct the newtype and read and write the struct's private fields, because Check counts the children's declarations among the module's own for construction and field rights; Resolve also lets a parent name a child's newtype as a symbol without an import. Two options: enforce TYPE-11 as written (a newtype or private field is constructed or accessed only inside the module that declares it, so a child is another module), or amend TYPE-11 to grant a parent these rights over the declarations it imports from its children | the behaviour dates from construction rights slice 1 (2026-07-18); it contradicts TYPE-11@1's wording ("outside the module that declares it"); bug 262's record names `tests/programs/module_qualified_impl` as a program that applying TYPE-11 strictly would refuse; the wider impact is not yet measured (no `scripts/tools/rule_impact.sh` scan is recorded); bug 262's record (branch `wip/newtype-ctor-stage`) leaves the question to R-0011 and makes no recommendation | R-0011; the construction-rights fixtures (`module_qualified_impl` and bug 262's `error_262_*` and `regress_262_*` fixtures); bug 262's follow-up (whether Resolve binds a child's newtype in its parent) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: only the declaring module; a newtype is constructed, and a private field read or written, only inside the module that declares it, and a parent module gets no rights over its child (TYPE-11 enforced as written) |
1319:| OD29 | <a id="od29"></a>OWN-7: a statement expression has type `Unit` (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | only a syntactically pure non-Unit Copy value is refused (E0294); a call's result is dropped silently; bug 325 | exit 2q; exit 2j | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): a statement expression must have type `Unit`; every other unused result needs `discard(e)`; the syntactic purity heuristic is removed. Now OWN-7@3 (partial, bug 325) |
1324:| OD34 | <a id="od34"></a>A struct literal inside a call's argument list, an index or an array literal within an `if`, `while` or `match` head (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD16): whether it counts as parenthesized under SYN-4. Two readings: only a grouped expression `( expr )` parenthesizes, so `if same(p, P { v: 1 }) { ... }` is refused; or any enclosing bracket does, so it is accepted (Rust's reading). Recommendation: any enclosing bracket, since inside a call's parentheses the head's `{` cannot be meant, and refusing it would require parentheses inside parentheses Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): only an enclosing `(` or `[` parenthesizes, stated as one invariant: in a condition head, a `{` at bracket depth zero opens the block | not in the record; the current compiler accepts it (the uppercase heuristic reads a struct literal anywhere in a head) | bug 318's repair (SYN-4); V1c | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Only enclosing `(`/`[` parenthesize; `{` at depth 0 opens the block |
1329:| OD39 | <a id="od39"></a>The interface artifact format (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD9). Options: a generated `.coni` text file per module beside the build output; a JSON interface in the build directory; or the `pub` declarations rendered as Concrete source. Recommendation: rendered Concrete source (declarations without bodies), deterministic and reviewable as a diff Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): the `pub` declarations rendered as Concrete source without bodies, generated by the compiler, with an API-diff gate; it also carries private layout, impl methods, attributes, constants, capabilities and generic bodies, and is not read as FFI-6 bodyless foreign bindings | the compiler has the declaration-level `FileSummary` and `--report interface`, but no persisted per-module interface artifact and no API-diff gate of the proposed form | R-0556 (MOD-7) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `pub` decls rendered as Concrete source, compiler-generated, API-diff gate |
1352:| OD62 | <a id="od62"></a>Unused values and variables (added 2026-10-30, item Q3 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether an unused local variable or parameter is refused (Go's rule) and whether an unused `Result` or `Option` may be dropped by `discard`. Options: (a) refuse only an unused `Result` or `Option` (OWN-6); (b) Go's rule: any unused local or parameter is refused (an intentionally unused parameter is named `_`), and `discard` refuses `Result` and `Option`. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b), stated as one invariant: every name a body binds is used or is `_` (a bodyless declaration has no body, so needs no exception); `discard` and wildcard drops refuse `Result` and `Option`, Copy or not; to clarify: `_` as a loop index, and an unused `&mut` binding under REF-10. Austral: not checked | unused Copy locals and parameters are accepted; OWN-6 refuses an unused fallible result | OWN-5, OWN-6, OWN-7, REF-10 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Every bound name used or `_`; `discard` refuses Result/Option |
1357:| OD67 | <a id="od67"></a>Locals that reuse a top-level name (added 2026-10-30, item review new 4 (SCOPE-4) of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a local binding may reuse the name of a top-level function, an import alias or an intrinsic (MEMORY_SEMANTICS §15.4 also lists constants). Options: (a) allowed, as today; (b) refused, as SCOPE-1 refuses shadowing a local. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: not checked | allowed | SCOPE-1, SCOPE-2, MOD-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
1369:| OD79 | <a id="od79"></a>`assert` in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C2 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains the proof-annotation statement `assert expr;`. Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): individual review required; no pick recorded. Austral: not checked | the parser accepts it | CON-2, CON-3, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Add `assert`. Extended 2026-11-06 by `assert(e) by thm(...)` ([SPARK row](#decided-project-spark-2026-11-06)) |
1381:| OD91 | <a id="od91"></a>A cast followed by `<` (added 2026-10-30, item LL(1) type\|5.2 of grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv). Question: How `x as T < y` parses, given that `<` after a cast's type name may start type arguments or be the less-than operator. Options: (a) a cast's type takes no type arguments unless parenthesized (`x as (T<U>)`), so `<` is less-than; (b) the parser's reading: `<` always starts type arguments, so the comparison needs parentheses (`(x as T) < y`); (c) refuse `<` directly after a cast's type and require parentheses either way. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (in the LL(1) ledger, not the picks). Austral: a conversion is the cast expression `e : T`, which takes no type arguments after it, so the conflict does not arise | the parser always reads type arguments, so `x as T < y` is refused (grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv, row type\|5.2) | SYN-1, SYN-2, ARITH-5 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `x as T < y` is less-than; a generic cast type needs parentheses `x as (Vec<T>)` |
1383:| OD93 | <a id="od93"></a>Widening a function value's capability set (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a function value converts to a function type whose capability set contains its own, implicitly by subset or by an explicit conversion. Bears on TYPE-4, TYPE-17, TRUST-4. The current compiler: refused (E0220): `fn(Int) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int`, nor `with(File)` a `with(Std)`, and there is no explicit conversion; a capability-polymorphic parameter accepts either by instantiating its variable. The callable-values design (§10) says there is no implicit widening, and that "widening is explicit or via lattice subsumption only", with a lattice example (`Concurrent ⊇ Async`) whose names are not capabilities; HANDLE_CAPABILITIES R7 keeps handle types invariant, as TYPE-4 does. Options: (a) exact types, as in the current compiler; (b) subsumption into a larger set; (c) an explicit conversion. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-93 | see OPEN-93 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Exact types **Canonical current capability-set rule: authority rule 4; this earlier exact-types answer refers to it.** |
1388:| OD98 | <a id="od98"></a>Pointer and reference conversions written with `as` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Which conversions between raw pointers and references `as` writes (`&x as *const T`, `p as &T`, `p as &mut T`, `p as *mut U`). Bears on TRUST-1, TRUST-3, REF-5, ARITH-5. The current compiler: `&x as *const T` is accepted in safe code, `p as &T` from a raw pointer is accepted in trusted or `Unsafe` code (the binary and `--interp` agree), and a reference cast between different pointee types (`a as &Int` with `a: &A`) is refused (E0553). No entry states these: TRUST-3 forms a reference from a raw pointer with `&*p`, and REF-5 lists borrowing and projection as the ways a reference is formed. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8 kept `as` "the spelling of pointer and reference casts (`as *const T`, `as *mut T`, `as &K`, `as &mut V`)", 281 lines of std. Options: (a) `as` keeps every one of them; (b) `as` converts only a reference to a raw pointer, and a reference is formed from a raw pointer only by `&*p` (TRUST-3); (c) pointer conversions become named functions. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-98 | see OPEN-98 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Reference to raw pointer conversion only in trusted/Unsafe code (equal to Austral) |
1389:| OD99 | <a id="od99"></a>The names of the named conversions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: The spellings of the named conversions of ARITH-5, ARITH-10 and ARITH-11 (`try_convert`, `truncate`, `reinterpret`, the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions, and the float roundings `trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`). Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10, ARITH-11. The current compiler: none of them exists (E0101). ARITH-5's sentence names `truncate` and `reinterpret`, while the archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 calls its spellings indicative, to be fixed by the API's owner; it names the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions by their rounding only. Options: (a) the spellings above, as builtins; (b) other names, or std functions rather than builtins. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-99 | see OPEN-99 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): These spellings, as builtins |
1393:| OD103 | <a id="od103"></a>`as` between reference types (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing MEMORY_SEMANTICS). Question: Whether `as` may convert a reference type to a reference type, given that REF-5 lists borrowing and projecting as the only operations that form a reference. Bears on REF-5, REF-6, ARITH-5, TRUST-4. The current compiler: an identity cast between equal reference types is accepted and denotes the operand's place (`read(r as &Int)` with `r: &Int`, and `read(&v as &Int)`, exit 42 on both consumers); every other cast between reference types, `&mut Int as &Int` included, is E0553 at core-check under ARITH-5's tag, whose sentence covers only numeric, `bool` and `char` conversions; a cast from a reference to a raw pointer is allowed (TRUST-4). The design record (MEMORY_SEMANTICS §15.3) listed a cast of a traced reference binding (`r as &U`) among the reference-forming operations. Options: (a) refuse `as` between reference types; (b) allow the identity cast as a no-op on the operand's place. Conversions between a reference and a raw pointer are OPEN-98's, not this question's. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the MEMORY_SEMANTICS absorption, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-103 | see OPEN-103 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
1396:| OD106 | <a id="od106"></a>The result type of a checked conversion (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a checked conversion (`try_convert`) reports a value it cannot preserve as `Option::None` or as a `Result` error carrying the reason. Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10. The current compiler: no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 wrote `try_convert::<T>(x) -> Option<T>` "(or a `Result` with a reason)" and left the choice to the API's owner. Options: (a) `Option<T>`; (b) a `Result` whose error names the reason (out of range, not integral, NaN, infinite, not representable). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500) | ARITH-5, ARITH-10 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `Result<T, OutOfRange>` (failure, not absence) |
1397:| OD107 | <a id="od107"></a>What a float-to-integer rounding gives for NaN, infinities and out-of-range values (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether each float-to-integer rounding operation (`trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`) gives a value or an error for NaN, an infinity or a result outside the target type's range, and which value. Bears on ARITH-1, ARITH-11. The current compiler: the rounding operations do not exist (R-0500); a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range, natively and in `--interp`. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 required a stated result for every input, "either as a value or as an error". Options: (a) an error, as `try_convert` reports; (b) a value: the nearest value of the type for an out-of-range input or an infinity, and 0 for NaN; (c) a trap, as `as` does today. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the rounding operations do not exist; a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range | ARITH-1, ARITH-11 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): An error, as `try_convert` |
1404:| OD114 | <a id="od114"></a>How integer division rounds (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: How an integer division that does not abort rounds its quotient, and which sign a nonzero remainder takes. Bears on ARITH-3. The current compiler: the quotient is truncated toward zero and a nonzero remainder has the sign of the dividend, as C99 and LLVM's `sdiv` and `srem` give (`-7 / 2` is `-3`, `-7 % 2` is `-1`, `7 % -2` is `1`), in the binary, the interpreter and the constant folder (`IntArith.evalIntBinOp`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) states when division aborts (§5) but not how it rounds. Options: (a) truncation toward zero, as in the current compiler; (b) floored division, the remainder taking the divisor's sign; (c) Euclidean division, the remainder never negative. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the quotient is truncated toward zero and a remainder has the dividend's sign (C99) | ARITH-3 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Truncation toward zero |
1408:| OD118 | <a id="od118"></a>A proven run-time violation in trusted code (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether an index the compiler proves out of bounds, or a division it proves to be by zero, is refused in `trusted` and `with(Unsafe)` code as in safe code. Bears on MEM-6, ARITH-3, TRUST-5, TRUST-12. The current compiler: `a[5]` on a `[Int; 3]`, or `x / 0`, is refused in safe code (E0900, `error[runtime-safety]`) and compiles in a `trusted fn` (the index also in a function declaring `Unsafe`), where it aborts at run time on both consumers. MEM-6 states the refusal with no exemption; [KNOWN_HOLES.md](../legacy/docs/verification/KNOWN_HOLES.md) C7 records the exemption as intended. Options: (a) refuse everywhere, as MEM-6 reads; (b) keep the exemption and state it in MEM-6. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-118 | see OPEN-118 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse everywhere **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): refusal of proven violations becomes refusal of unproven safety, everywhere.** |
1413:| OD123 | <a id="od123"></a>`as` on a newtype outside its module (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether `v as N` and `n as T`, between a newtype `N` and its inner type `T`, are accepted outside the module that declares `N`, where TYPE-11 refuses `N(v)`. Bears on TYPE-11, TYPE-24, ARITH-5, OPEN-54. The current compiler: both accepted in any module, so `x as Id` builds a foreign newtype that `Id(x)` may not (E0296), and a validated wrapper's smart constructor can be bypassed. [VALIDATED_WRAPPERS.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/VALIDATED_WRAPPERS.md) §8 describes the cast rule as guarding the wrapper contract from an `as` bypass, which holds for casts to unrelated types only. Options: (a) accepted anywhere, as in the current compiler; (b) accepted only where `N(v)` and `.0` are (TYPE-11, with R-0500's follow-on for `.0`); (c) `as` never names a newtype, the conversions being `N(v)` and `.0`. Austral: no newtype (OPEN-54's comparison). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-123 | see OPEN-123 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `as` never names a newtype |
1416:| OD126 | <a id="od126"></a>Target assumptions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Which target facts are language rules: 64-bit pointers, little-endian byte order, POSIX hosts, and the sizes of `bool`, `Unit` and pointers. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-7, TYPE-19, TYPE-20. The current compiler: one target profile: `Layout` hardcodes 8-byte pointers and references, `bool` of 1 byte, `Unit` of 0 bytes, an `i32` enum tag, and assumes little-endian POSIX hosts; FFI-2 names AAPCS64 for the native profile, and the integer types' widths follow from their names (TYPE-19). [EXECUTION_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/EXECUTION_MODEL.md) and [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) state these as platform facts. Options: (a) language rules, a 32-bit or big-endian target being another language profile; (b) target facts, observable only through `sizeof`, `alignof` (FFI-7) and named target constants; (c) as (b), with the sizes of `bool` and `Unit` fixed by the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-126 | see OPEN-126 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Language rules; other targets are other profiles. Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): profiles are read-only and separate address width, pointer representation, length width, byte order and address spaces; the existing 64-bit little-endian profile and its `i32` enum tags are unchanged |
1439:| OD149 | <a id="od149"></a>How `[e; N]` evaluates `e` (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: How many times an array repetition `[e; N]` evaluates `e`, and which `e` it accepts. Bears on STMT-4, OWN-15 and TYPE-7. The current compiler: `e` is evaluated once per element, in index order (`[bump(&mut n); 3]` gives `[1, 2, 3]` on both consumers), and whether a linear `e` is accepted was not shown (an array of a linear type that is never consumed is refused with E0208 either way). OWN-15 says only that an array literal moves each element in, and STMT-4 orders written elements. Options: (a) `e` is evaluated once and copied, so it must be Copy; (b) `e` is evaluated `N` times in index order, as the current compiler does; (c) `e` must be a constant expression. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-149 | see OPEN-149 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Evaluated once, must be Copy |
1445:| OD155 | <a id="od155"></a>`unwrap` beside TYPE-24's conversions (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether `unwrap` stays an intrinsic beside the conversions TYPE-24 lists (`n.0` and `n as T`), becomes one of those conversions, or is removed. Bears on TYPE-24, TYPE-11, TYPE-10 and OPEN-54. The current compiler: Check gives `unwrap(m)` the newtype's inner type (E0270 when the argument is not a newtype); `return unwrap(m);` exits 42 on both consumers, and `let x: Int = unwrap(m);` followed by `if x == 42` fails the native build at SSA verification (E0715, bug 345). TYPE-24 lists `n.0` and `n as T` and does not list `unwrap`. OPEN-54 decides this only if its answer removes newtypes; while newtypes stay, this question stays. Options: (a) keep `unwrap` as an intrinsic and leave it off TYPE-24's list, as the current compiler and TYPE-24 do; (b) `unwrap(n)` is a conversion, TYPE-24 lists it, and TYPE-11 restricts it as it restricts `n.0`; (c) remove `unwrap`, leaving `n.0` and `n as T`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-155 | see OPEN-155 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No builtin `unwrap`; use `match` |
