186:- **TYPE-22@1's examples** (row Q6.4): a constant whose initializer provably fails is refused at compile time whether or not it is used (OD118); both examples, `unused-constant-never-runs` and `constant-traps-at-use`, become reject examples. When a constant is evaluated (at each use, never at start) is unchanged.
190:- **The text-only slice of R4 F4.3** (row Q4.7) is replaced before it was recorded: one scoped form opens every view, `borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]`. Without `else` an invalid range traps, like indexing; with `else` it is handled; a proven invalid range without `else` is refused (OD118). Arrays need `a ≤ b ≤ len`; text also needs UTF-8 scalar boundaries at both byte offsets.
191:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields"></a>**Q6.2 and R4 F12, known values through structs** (owner-approved revision, 2026-11-05, made during A2b): known values propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring. The checker tracks an abstract value for each binding: unknown, a known scalar, or a constructor whose fields carry their own abstract values. Constructing a struct records each field's fact independently; an immutable binding keeps the fact; destructuring hands each field's fact, recursively, to the corresponding immutable binder; an unknown component stays unknown without discarding its known siblings; an ownership transfer keeps the facts for the receiving binding without permitting reuse of the consumed one. Q6.2's exclusions stay (mutable state, branches and paths, user-function calls, calls outside the table, elaborator temporaries). So `let S { a } = S { a: 200u8 }; a + 100` is refused, in trusted and unreachable code too (OD118 as revised by Q6.4). Before this revision a field bound by destructuring was not known and that program was accepted and trapped.
212:- **Q2.2** Which containers implement `Destroy`? — Exactly sixteen named Destroy impls: Array (T: Destroy); Heap, HeapArray and Vec (two each: T: Copy; T: Destroy); String (unconditional); Option (T: Destroy); Result (three: T: Destroy/E: Copy, T: Destroy/E: Destroy, T: Copy/E: Destroy); OrderedMap (four: each of K and V Copy or Destroy). HashMap excluded. <a id="q2-2-revision"></a>Revised 2026-11-06 by [information flow](#decided-project-infoflow-2026-11-06): `Secret<T>` is `Copy` only for scalar `T`; secret arrays and heap secrets are linear and their destruction wipes them.
225:- **Q3.7** What is the raw-memory model and validity taxonomy? — Blocks with provenance; pointer = (prov: Option<BlockId>, addr: u64); integers carry no provenance; every access needs explicit validity premises. *Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): the address is read from a read-only target profile; the existing 64-bit little-endian profile is unchanged, and the provenance rules and the zero-byte exception are preserved.*
231:- **Q4.1** What is a literal’s type? — Copy `StaticStr`; explicit fallible dynamic conversion.
