legacy/docs/spec/types.md:1498:### TYPE-22. A constant is evaluated where it is used
legacy/docs/spec/types.md:1500:**TYPE-22@1.** A module constant's initializer is evaluated at each use of the constant, in a scope of its own that sees no local of the using function, and never at program start, so the initializer of a constant that is not used never runs.
legacy/docs/spec/types.md:1511:**Revised by the owner 2026-11-05** ([row Q6.4](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-revisions)): a constant whose initializer provably fails is refused at compile time whether or not it is used (OD118). Both examples below, `unused-constant-never-runs` and `constant-traps-at-use`, become reject examples when this rule is written in Lean; until then the revision governs over them. When an initializer runs (at each use, never at start) is unchanged.
legacy/docs/spec/types.md:1513:**Rationale.** A constant has no storage: the binary inlines its initializer at each use and the
