ROADMAP.md:415:| R-0603 | Rule revisions, the retired-ID registry and `spec_version` | each Lean rule has its own revision, bumped when its meaning changes, including through dependencies, and checked; retired rule IDs stay in a registry; every output carries one `spec_version` header covering every record (0.x before the freeze, 1.0 at A2c, bumped by every normative change), checked in CI and distinct from the product version | R-0571 |
ROADMAP.md:418:| R-0609 | Foundation C3: target, layout and representation ([contract](docs/proposals/FOUNDATION.md#c3-target-layout-and-representation)) | the read-only target profile (address width, pointer storage, length width, byte order, address spaces, maximum object sizes) and the layout interface are in Lean, with the existing 64-bit little-endian profile as an instance ([question 3](docs/decisions.md#foundation-q3)); the layout laws (alignment, size, in-bounds non-overlapping fields, unique discriminants, valid tag decoding) are proved for the native, C and packed algorithms; `i32` enum tags, no niches, `Secret<T>` with `T`'s layout; `rep_roundtrip` is restated over it and `Rep` is deleted; Home: spec (`Target`, `Layout`, `Values`). Gate: Spec build, the rule gate and the proof-integrity jobs | R-0608 |
ROADMAP.md:445:| R-0581 | BIR as a complete backend contract shared by LLVM and the LCB ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-proof-infrastructure), 2026-11-06) | BIR's Lean definition fixes integer semantics, layout and endianness, alignment, zero-sized values, internal and foreign calling conventions, external events, trap realization (kind and site) and a frame-accounting handoff; no BIR construct, invariant or lowering choice depends on one backend; stack bytes are never derived from BIR slot counts, only from allocated frames in the final artifact; the slice-0 instance is delivered at B0 | R-0496, R-0502 |
ROADMAP.md:474:| R-0272 | Restart fuzzing against the new compiler on concrete-ci-2, including the random-program differential against the Lean evaluator from the slice-0 front end ([early decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence)), seeded from the regressions and examples that survive in the repository and from newly generated programs (the ci-3 corpus and findings were not copied before the handover, decision row P14, and are lost), with reduction and counterexample-to-regression persistence | the random-program differential starts with the slice-0 front end and grows one feature per slice; the generators emit slice-supported programs, the oracle is Lean semantics against native code, and findings become regressions; legacy fuzzing stopped at the move; Home: tooling. Gate: the fuzzer, short in CI and long nightly on concrete-ci-2 ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0425 |
ROADMAP.md:577:| R-0558 | Move the old tree to `legacy/` in one train and set up the minimal root. Day one, the root holds the README, this file, AGENTS.md, an empty CHANGELOG, LICENSE, the new build files, `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean`, `docs/PHILOSOPHY.md`, `docs/decisions.md`, the landing, review and hook scripts, `scripts/oracle/`, `required-checks.txt` and its job mapping, `RETENTION.tsv`, the minimal issue record `ISSUES.tsv` with its validator, and the [2026-11 roadmap archive](docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/README.md) with its task disposition table. Everything else comes back from `legacy/` when a slice needs it. The status page, the work-queue dispatcher, the fuzz farm, perf tracking and the nightly campaign stop, and each is listed in `RETENTION.tsv` as archived; each returns only with a roadmap task. The ci-3 fuzz corpus, perf history and leanprof data were not copied before the handover (decision row P14) and are not archived. | the root holds only that set; a root check refuses any modification or addition under `legacy/`, and any deletion its `RETENTION.tsv` row does not allow (controls for each); no legacy gate runs; the move lands on both remotes | T0, R-0563, R-0565, owner: approval of the move inventory |
